          %h3#characterization_as_eilenbergmoore_algebras Characterization as Eilenberg-Moore algebras
          %p
            Orthogonal factorization systems are equivalently described by the (appropriately defined)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+algebras"} Eilenberg-Moore algebras
            with respect to the monad which belongs to the endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K} \mapsto \mathcal{K}^2
            of (the 2-category)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            (
            %a{:href => "#KorostenskiTholen"}> Korostenski-Tholen, Thrm B
            ).
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            Several classical examples of OFS
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,M)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \= class of all epis,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \= class of all monos: the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28epi%2C+mono%29+factorization+system"} (epi, mono) factorization system
            %li
              %p
                more generally, in any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \= class of all
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+epimorphisms"} regular epimorphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \= class of all monos
            %li
              %p
                in any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \= all epimorphisms,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \= all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphisms"} strong monomorphisms
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \=
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functors"} identity-on-objects functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \=
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functors"} fully faithful functors
                (this is furthermore a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+factorisation+system"} strict factorisation system
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \=
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bo+functors"} bo functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \=
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functors"} fully faithful functors
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bo-ff+factorization+system"} bo-ff factorization system
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \=
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functors"} final functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \=
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibrations"} discrete fibrations
                (This is called the
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comprehensive+factorization+system"} comprehensive factorization system
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \=
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+functors"} initial functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \=
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+opfibration"} discrete opfibration
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \=
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ULF+functors"} ULF functors
                (see there for a reference)
            %li
              %p
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \=
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative+functor"} conservative functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \= left orthogonal of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                (“iterated strict localizations” after A. Joyal)
            %li
              %p
                in the category of small categories where morphisms are functors which are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} left exact
                and have
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \= class of all such functors which are also localizations,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \= class of all such functors which are also conservative
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\to C
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered category
                in the sense of Grothendieck, then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                admits a factorization system
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,M)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                \= arrows whose projection to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is invertible,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                \= cartesian arrows in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %li
              %p
                See the (
                %a{:href => "#Joyal"}> catlab
                ) for more examples.
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorified
            notion of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system+on+a+2-category"} factorization system on a 2-category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28eso%2C+fully+faithful%29+factorization+system"} (eso, fully faithful) factorization system
            %li
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28eso%2Bfull%2C+faithful%29+factorization+system"} (eso+full, faithful) factorization system
          %p
            Similarly, we can have a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} factorization system in an (∞,1)-category
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system"} factorization system
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
                %li
                  %p
                    %strong orthogonal factorization system
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+factorization+system"} strict factorization system
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+factorization+system"} reflective factorization system
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+factorization+system"} stable factorization system
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system+in+a+2-category"} factorization system in a 2-category
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+factorization+system"} enhanced factorization system
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} factorization system in an (∞,1)-category
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} orthogonal factorization system in an (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system+in+a+derivator"} orthogonal factorization system in a derivator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+structure+for+sinks"} factorization structure for sinks
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Factorisation systems appear to have been first studied by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mac+Lane"} Mac Lane
            in the following paper under the term
            %em bicategory
            (not to be confused with
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              \.
              = succeed "." do
                %em Duality for groups
          %p The definition of orthogonal factorisation system essentially appears under the name “factorization” in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"} Max Kelly
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories of continuous functors
              %strong 2
              (1972) 169-191 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(72)90001-1"}> doi:10.1016/0022-4049(72)90001-1
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p and under the name “factorization system” in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aldridge+Bousfield"}> Aldridge Bousfield
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Constructions of factorization systems in categories
              %strong 9
              2-3 (1977) 207-220 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(77)90067-6"}> doi:10.1016/0022-4049(77)90067-6
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Joyal
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/joyalscatlab/published/Factorization+systems"} Factorization systems
            %li#KorostenskiTholen
              %p
                Mareli Korostenski,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
                %em
                  Factorization systems as Eilenberg-Moore algebras, (
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049%2893%2990171-O"}> doi
                  )
            %li#Grandis01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"}> Marco Grandis
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the monad of proper factorisation systems in categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-4049%2801%2900114-1"} doi
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0101154"} arxiv
          %p Introductory texts:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory"} Introduction to Homotopy Theory
            %li#Riehl2008
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/factorization.pdf"}
                  %em Factorization Systems
            %li
              The following dissertation section is entirely written after learning from Riehl’s note above, but has complementary examples and may dive deeper into some proofs:
              %ul
                %li#Nuyts2020
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Nuyts"}> Andreas Nuyts
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Contributions to Multimode and Presheaf Type Theory, section 2.4: Factorization Systems
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://lirias.kuleuven.be/retrieve/581985"} PhD thesis
          %p
            A connection to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+categories"} double categories
            may be found in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Miloslav Štěpán,
                %em Factorization systems and double categories
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06714"}> arXiv:2305.06714
                \&amp;rbrack;.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Branko+Juran"}> Branko Juran
                ,
                %em On orthogonal factorization systems and double categories
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.01363"}> arXiv:2501.01363
                \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
